            <div xml:id="d13">

                <pb facs="0109" n="13" sameAs="frus:frus1872p1/pg13"/>

                <head>No. 13.</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1872-07-15"><hi rendition="#italic">July</hi> 15,
                        1872</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center">[<hi rendition="#small-caps">Circular No</hi>. 24.]</p>
                <p rendition="#flush"><hi rendition="#italic">To the United States Consul
                    at</hi>—.</p>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: A copy of an act of Congress approved by
                    the President 7th June, 1872, entitled “An act to authorize the appointment of
                    shipping-commissioners by the several circuit courts of the United States, to
                    superintend the shipping and discharge of seamen engaged in merchant ships
                    belonging to the United States, and for the further protection of seamen,” is
                    subjoined to this circular, and your attention is invited to its provisions,
                    especially those contained in sections fifteen, thirty-six, thirty-eight,
                    forty-four, and forty-six, and in the note appended to Table D.</p>
                <p>The act is appointed to take effect in sixty days after its passage, that is to
                    say, on the 6th of August, 1872.</p>
                <p>This circular is addressed to all consuls-general, consuls, and commercial agents
                    of the United States, although few, if any, of its provisions will require
                    action on the part of consular officers in inland places.</p>
                <p>You will report to the Department the date at which you receive this circular,
                    and you will be careful to communicate to the consular agents within your
                    jurisdiction such information as will enable them to discharge <pb facs="0110"
                        n="14" xml:id="pg14"/> any duty that may become incumbent on them by the
                    passage of this act.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>CHARLES HALE,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Assistant
                        Secretary.</hi></signed>
                </closer>


                <xi:include href="d13-a1.xml" xpointer="d13-a1"/>
